  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Total federal contract obligations are $502B for FY2026 to date. These totals are partial, fiscal year to date, and represent contract obligations rather than full-year spending. The Department of Defense is the largest single buyer with $260B in obligations. The next largest agencies are the Department of Homeland Security at $53B and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $51.8B. The Department of Energy follows with $46B. Together the top buyers show where most federal contracting dollars are concentrated so far in the fiscal year, while smaller buyers include the General Services Administration at $19.4B and the Department of Health and Human Services at $14.3B. Other notable agencies are NASA at $10.1B, the Department of Transportation at $9.1B, the Department of Agriculture at $6.7B, and the Department of Justice at $6.6B. Treat these figures as year-to-date obligations, not full fiscal year totals.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest federal buyers of contracts?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the biggest buyer, with $260B in obligations. The Department of Homeland Security follows with $53B, then the Department of Veterans Affairs with $51.8B, and the Department of Energy with $46B. Those four agencies account for the largest shares of the $502B in obligations to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How spread out is contract spending across agencies?",
        "body": "Contract obligations concentrate at the top but extend down the list of agencies. After the top four, the General Services Administration has $19.4B and the Department of Health and Human Services has $14.3B, with NASA at $10.1B and smaller totals for Transportation, Agriculture, and Justice. All figures are fiscal year to date obligations and partial for the current year."
      }
    ]
  },
